<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masaccio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1401">1401</a>–<a href="/years/1428">1428</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masaccio (1401–1428) was a Florentine painter, the first great painter of the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. In a short career he applied Brunelleschi's linear perspective and a new sculptural naturalism in works such as the Holy Trinity fresco in Santa Maria Novella and the frescoes of the Brancacci Chapel, which later artists including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> studied. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the age of 26.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masaccio.jpg" alt="Masaccio" width="290" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Masaccio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masaccio_Self_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Masaccio was born Tommaso di Ser Giovanni di Simone Cassai on 21 December 1401 at Castel San Giovanni di Altura, now San Giovanni Valdarno in Tuscany. His father was a notary, who died in 1406, and his mother the daughter of an innkeeper. The name Masaccio is a humorous form of Maso, short for Tommaso, meaning clumsy or messy Tom, and may have been coined to tell him apart from his collaborator Masolino.</p><p>There is no evidence of his training. He is first documented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 7 January 1422, when he joined the painters' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> as an independent master. His first attributed works are the San Giovenale Triptych of 1422 and the Virgin and Child with Saint Anne of about 1424, painted with the older Masolino da Panicale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1424 Felice Brancacci commissioned Masaccio and Masolino to paint frescoes in the Brancacci Chapel in Santa Maria del Carmine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and work began around 1425. Most scenes show the life of Saint Peter, with the temptation and expulsion of Adam and Eve at the entrance. Masolino left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in September 1425, and Masaccio left the cycle unfinished in 1426 and returned in 1427. Filippino Lippi completed it in the 1480s. The Expulsion from the Garden of Eden influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, and in The Tribute Money the shadows of the figures fall away from the chapel window as if it lit them.</p><p>Around 1427 he painted the Holy Trinity for Santa Maria Novella in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, considered by many his masterwork and the earliest surviving painting to use systematic linear perspective, possibly devised with the help of Brunelleschi. A skeleton below the figures bears the inscription: I once was what now you are, and what I am you shall yet be.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Masaccio died in 1428 at the age of 26, and little is known of the circumstances. A legend says a jealous rival painter poisoned him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a> said on hearing the news: We have suffered a great loss. Only four frescoes undoubtedly by his hand survive. He is considered to have begun the Early Italian Renaissance in painting by moving away from the ornament of International Gothic towards naturalism, perspective and chiaroscuro. Vasari wrote that the most distinguished painters from his time on studied the Brancacci Chapel, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masaccio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…century Filippo Brunelleschi’s dome of Florence Cathedral, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of Donatello and the painting of <b>Masaccio</b> launched the artistic Renaissance, followed by Sandro Botticelli, Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo, whose David was set up outside the Palazzo Vecchio in 1504.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Giovanni Valdarno, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.56,11.53&amp;year=1418" aria-label="San Giovanni Valdarno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4827 619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Masaccio"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5277 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5277 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.56,11.53&amp;year=1418">What was here in 1418? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
